==============================================================================
PART 6  the exact content of contiguity: the Mellin ladder  [PROVED]
==============================================================================
By THM-3012 addendum 2, S(k) = M(1/k)/k where M is the single Mellin
transform
        M(s) = int_0^1 z^{s-1} 2F1(1/4,3/4;1;z) dz = sum_{n>=0} c_n/(n+s),
        c_n  = (1/4)_n (3/4)_n/(n!)^2 = Gamma(n+1/4)Gamma(n+3/4)/(pi sqrt2 (n!)^2).

THEOREM (PROVED here).  For every s with Re s > 0,

        (s + 1/4)(s + 3/4) M(s+1)  =  s^2 M(s)  +  1/(pi sqrt2).            (L)

PROOF.  c_{n+1}(n+1)^2 = c_n (n+1/4)(n+3/4).  Divide the numerator by n+s+1:
        (n+1/4)(n+3/4) = (n+s+1)(n-s) + (s^2 + s + 3/16).
Summing n = 0..N-1 on the left and m = 1..N on the right, the two copies of
sum c_n (n-s) -- individually divergent -- cancel, leaving the boundary terms
        -s - c_N(N-s)  and  -s^2/s.
Since c_N ~ 1/(pi sqrt2 N), c_N(N-s) -> 1/(pi sqrt2), and (L) follows.  QED

(L) IS the contiguous relation of the family, in its sharpest form: a
FIRST-ORDER inhomogeneous recurrence in the Mellin exponent, with rational
coefficients and inhomogeneity 1/(pi sqrt2).  Its homogeneous solution is
        h(s) = Gamma(s)^2 / (Gamma(s+1/4) Gamma(s+3/4)),
so the general solution has exactly ONE free constant per ladder s + Z.

CONSEQUENCE 1 (the k = 1 ladder is Gauss-anchored).  On the integer ladder,
M(1) = S(1) = 8 sqrt2/(3 pi) is a Gauss value, so with r_m := M(m) pi/sqrt2,
        r_1 = 8/3,   r_{m+1} = 16 (m^2 r_m + 1/2) / ((4m+1)(4m+3)),
and EVERY integer moment is rational times sqrt2/pi:
   m = 1 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 8/3                       |numeric - exact| = 5.346e-51
   m = 2 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 152/105                   |numeric - exact| = 2.673e-51
   m = 3 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 10568/10395               |numeric - exact| = 2.673e-51
   m = 4 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 178328/225225             |numeric - exact| = 1.336e-51
   m = 5 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 47453768/72747675         |numeric - exact| = 0.0
   m = 6 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 782539544/1405485081      |numeric - exact| = 1.336e-51
   m = 7 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 51331850888/105411381075  |numeric - exact| = 0.0
   m = 8 :  M(m) pi/sqrt2 = 838519635608/1933976154825  |numeric - exact| = 6.682e-52
   ladder control: PASS

CONSEQUENCE 2 (why k >= 2 is different).  For k >= 2 the ladder through
s = 1/k contains no integer, hence no Gauss point: (L) relates S(k) only to
the values M(1/k + j), j >= 1, which are NOT members of the S-family.  The
recurrence therefore propagates S(k) but can never COMPUTE it.  This is the
exact and complete answer to 'does the contiguous configuration reduce S(4)
to Gauss 2F1(1) values': it does so precisely on the ladder 1/k in Z, i.e.
k = 1, and on no other.

numerical confirmation of (L) at several s, including the S-family points:
   s = 1                residual of (L) = 5.834e-62
   s = 2                residual of (L) = 5.834e-62
   s = 1/2 (k=2)        residual of (L) = 1.945e-62
   s = 1/3 (k=3)        residual of (L) = 5.834e-62
   s = 1/4 (k=4)        residual of (L) = 1.945e-62
   s = 1/5 (k=5)        residual of (L) = 3.889e-62
   s = 1/7 (k=7)        residual of (L) = 0.0
   s = 0.3141592653     residual of (L) = 1.945e-62
   (L) control: PASS

